Sure, here's a sample Java code for simulating the experiment using Bernoulli Distribution:
```java
import java.util.Random;
public class BernoulliExperiment {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] frequency = new int[10];
        Random random = new Random();
        int numTrials = 10;

        for (int i = 0; i < numTrials; i++) {
            int ballNum = random.nextInt(10) + 1;
            frequency[ballNum - 1]++;
        }

        System.out.println("Frequency of each ball number selected:");
        for (int i = 0; i < frequency.length; i++) {
            System.out.println("Ball " + (i + 1) + ": " + frequency[i] + " times");
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, we first create an array `frequency` to keep track of the frequency of each ball number selected. We then create a `Random` object to generate random numbers. The variable `numTrials` is set to 10 to simulate the experiment being repeated 10 times.
We use a `for` loop to repeat the experiment 10 times. In each trial, we generate a random number between 1 and 10 using `random.nextInt(10) + 1`, and then increment the corresponding element in `frequency`.
Finally, we use another `for` loop to print out the frequency of each ball number selected. We iterate through the `frequency` array and print out each element along with the corresponding ball number.

