Sure, here's an optimized implementation of QuickSort algorithm using C#:
```
public static List<int> QuickSort(List<int> input)
{
    if (input.Count <= 1)
        return input;
    int pivotIndex = input.Count / 2;
    int pivotValue = input[pivotIndex];
    input.RemoveAt(pivotIndex);
    List<int> less = new List<int>();
    List<int> greater = new List<int>();
    foreach (int element in input)
    {
        if (element <= pivotValue)
            less.Add(element);
        else
            greater.Add(element);
    }
    List<int> sorted = new List<int>();
    sorted.AddRange(QuickSort(less));
    sorted.Add(pivotValue);
    sorted.AddRange(QuickSort(greater));
    return sorted;
}
```
This implementation uses the middle element as the pivot and divides the input list into two sublists: one containing elements smaller than or equal to the pivot and another containing elements greater than the pivot. It then recursively applies the same steps to each sublist until they become empty. Finally, it merges the sorted sublists in the order of (sorted sublist with elements smaller than pivot) + (sorted sublist with elements equal to pivot) + (sorted sublist with elements greater than pivot).
Here are some examples of how to use the function with different input sizes and types of data:
```
List<int> input1 = new List<int>() { 3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3 };
List<int> sorted1 = QuickSort(input1);
// sorted1: { 1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 4, 5, 5, 6, 9 }
List<int> input2 = new List<int>() { 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 };
List<int> sorted2 = QuickSort(input2);
// sorted2: { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 }
List<int> input3 = new List<int>() { 1, 1, 1, 1, 1 };
List<int> sorted3 = QuickSort(input3);
// sorted3: { 1, 1, 1, 1, 1 }
List<int> input4 = new List<int>() { };
List<int> sorted4 = QuickSort(input4);
// sorted4: { }
List<int> input5 = new List<int>() { 5 };
List<int> sorted5 = QuickSort(input5);
// sorted5: { 5 }
```
In the worst-case scenario where the input list is already sorted in ascending or descending order, the pivot selection algorithm used in this implementation can cause the algorithm to degenerate to O(n^2) time complexity. To avoid this, you can use a different pivot selection algorithm, such as selecting the first or last element as the pivot, or randomly selecting an element as the pivot.

