What is the C++ code to identify the type of question in a given sentence? Specifically, if I have the sentence "Do you like pizza?", what code can I use to determine that it is a yes/no question?
Here is an example code in C++ using the Stanford CoreNLP library:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include "stanfordnlp/parser.h"
using namespace std;
using namespace stanfordnlp;
int main() {
  // initialize the parser
  Parser parser;
  parser.initialize();
  // input sentence
  string sentence = "Do you like pizza?";
  // parse the sentence
  auto document = parser.parse(sentence);
  // get the root of the parse tree
  auto root = document.sentence(0).parse_tree().root();
  // check if the root node is a verb with the tag VB or VBP (present tense verb)
  bool isYesNoQuestion = root->tag() == "VB" || root->tag() == "VBP";
  // print the result
  if (isYesNoQuestion) {
    cout << "The sentence is a yes/no question." << endl;
  } else {
    cout << "The sentence is not a yes/no question." << endl;
  }
  return 0;
}
This code uses the Stanford CoreNLP library to parse the input sentence and extract the root node of the parse tree. It then checks if the root node is a present tense verb, which is typically used in yes/no questions. If the root node is a verb, the code determines that the sentence is a yes/no question.

